{
  "schema_version": "1.7.0",
  "id": "USN-8932-1",
  "summary": "curl vulnerabilities",
  "details": "Stanislav Fort discovered that curl did not properly clear AWS SigV4\nauthentication state when CURLOPT_AWS_SIGV4 was set to NULL. An attacker\ncould possibly use this issue to obtain sensitive information. This\nissue only affected Ubuntu 22.04 LTS and Ubuntu 24.04 LTS.\n(CVE-2026-86409)\n\nStanislav Fort discovered that an HTTP proxy could trick libcurl into\nsending credentials to the proxy itself by responding to a CONNECT\nrequest with an unexpected authentication challenge. An attacker could\npossibly use this issue to obtain sensitive information.\n(CVE-2026-86414)\n\nStanislav Fort discovered that curl did not correctly handle HSTS\nentries when an includeSubDomains parent entry was imported before a\nlonger-lived child entry. An attacker could possibly use this issue to\ncause curl to use unencrypted HTTP connections. This issue only affected\nUbuntu 22.04 LTS and Ubuntu 24.04 LTS. (CVE-2026-87862)\n\nStanislav Fort discovered that curl did not properly handle HSTS max-age\nvalues that exceeded calendar time structure bounds. An attacker could\npossibly use this issue to cause a denial of service or data loss. This\nissue only affected Ubuntu 24.04 LTS. (CVE-2026-87863)\n\nStanislav Fort discovered that curl did not properly handle IMAP APPEND\ncommands when CURLOPT_CRLF was enabled, allowing overflow bytes to be\ninterpreted as pipelined commands. An authenticated attacker could\npossibly use this issue to inject arbitrary IMAP commands.\n(CVE-2026-87864)\n\nJoshua Rogers discovered that libcurl had a use-after-free vulnerability\nin the FTP wildcard matching code. An attacker could possibly use this\nissue to cause curl to crash, resulting in a denial of service, or\nexecute arbitrary code. (CVE-2026-92388)\n\nJoshua Rogers discovered that libcurl's TFTP state machine did not\nproperly validate transfer directions, allowing a malicious server to\nforce the client into transmit mode. An attacker could possibly use this\nissue to obtain sensitive information. (CVE-2026-92389)\n\nJoshua Rogers discovered that curl had a stack overflow vulnerability\nwhen processing deeply nested MIME structures. An attacker could\npossibly use this issue to cause curl to crash, resulting in a denial of\nservice. This issue only affected Ubuntu 18.04 LTS, Ubuntu 20.04 LTS,\nUbuntu 22.04 LTS and Ubuntu 24.04 LTS. (CVE-2026-92391)\n\nJoshua Rogers discovered that libcurl could expose credentials in the\nURL returned by CURLINFO_EFFECTIVE_URL when credentials were retrieved\nfrom a .netrc file. An attacker could possibly use this issue to obtain\nsensitive information. This issue only affected Ubuntu 20.04 LTS, Ubuntu\n22.04 LTS and Ubuntu 24.04 LTS. (CVE-2026-94266)",
